<div class="article-body">
    <Fragment set:html={`<h2>What Is a Mixed Grip Hang</h2>
      <p>A mixed grip dead hang places one hand overhand (pronated) and the other underhand (supinated) on the bar. The uneven hands can make the body want to turn. You may feel the trunk working to stay straight. This has not been measured.</p>
      <p>A mixed grip hang is an optional variation. The main hang training should stay double overhand.</p>
      <p>Powerlifters use a mixed grip on a loaded barbell to stop the bar rolling. A fixed pull-up bar does not roll. That benefit does not carry over to hanging.</p>
      <p>In a straight-arm hang both hands grip mainly with the finger flexors. The underhand arm may recruit the biceps more; no study of mixed-grip hangs was found.</p>

      <h2>Benefits</h2>
      <p>Mixed grip hangs offer variety and a mild trunk challenge. Evidence for specific benefits is limited. Treat them as an optional variation.</p>
      <h3>Trunk Steadiness</h3>
      <p>The uneven grip can make the body want to turn. You may feel your obliques and deep abdominal muscles working to keep you straight. A standard overhand hang does not have this pull.</p>

      <h3>Equal Work Across Both Arms</h3>
      <p>Alternating which hand is over and which is under every set gives both arms equal time in each position. Equal volume on both sides keeps the training even.</p>

      <h3>Grip Variety</h3>
      <p>A different hand position gives the grip a slightly different task.</p>
      <p>Add mixed grip hangs to your rotation alongside <a href="/passive-vs-active-hang/">passive and active hangs</a> for variety. If your <a href="/grip-strength/#grip-fails-first">grip gives out first</a>, fix that before adding variations.</p>

      <h3>Familiar Deadlift Hand Position</h3>
      <p>The mixed grip dead hang uses the same hand position as many heavy conventional deadlifts. Lifters who deadlift with a mixed grip may find the hang a familiar position. No study of mixed-grip hangs and deadlift grip was found.</p>

      <h2>Technique</h2>
      <p>Grip the bar at shoulder width with one hand overhand and one hand underhand. Wrap your thumbs around the bar on both hands. A full thumb wrap gives a more secure hold than a thumbless grip.</p>
      <p>Step off the platform with control. Keep your arms straight and let your full bodyweight hang. You may feel a pull to turn through your trunk. Resist it by bracing your trunk so your body hangs straight without twisting.</p>
      <p>Maintain square shoulders. Keep the shoulder blades down and the shoulders level so one side does not hunch.</p>
      <p>Breathe steadily and avoid holding your breath.</p>

      <div class="callout">
        <h3>Form Checklist</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>One hand pronated and one hand supinated</li>
          <li>Shoulder-width grip with full thumb wrap</li>
          <li>Arms straight with no elbow bend</li>
          <li>Trunk braced with no twisting</li>
          <li>Shoulders square and level</li>
          <li>Steady breathing</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <h2>When to Use Mixed Grip</h2>
      <h3>Training Variety</h3>
      <p>Rotating between overhand, underhand and mixed grip across the training week adds variety and spreads load across different hand positions. Move back to the other grips if any one irritates your wrist or elbow.</p>

      <h3>Deadlift Days</h3>
      <p>Do mixed grip hangs after heavy deadlifts or on a separate day. Do not use them as a warm-up. A hang before heavy pulls tires the grip you need for them.</p>

      <h3>Core Training Integration</h3>
      <p>Mixed grip hangs can sit in the same session as anti-rotation exercises such as side planks and Pallof presses.</p>

      <h2>Programming</h2>
      <p>Alternate which hand is overhand and which is underhand every set. Set 1 has your right hand over. Set 2 has your left hand over. This keeps both sides equal and avoids the lopsided pattern of always using the same mixed grip configuration.</p>

      <div class="callout">
        <h3>Mixed Grip Hang Protocol</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Sets:</strong> 3 per session (alternating hand position each set)</li>
          <li><strong>Time:</strong> 20 to 40 seconds per set</li>
          <li><strong>Rest:</strong> 60 to 90 seconds between sets</li>
          <li><strong>Frequency:</strong> Include it in 1 or 2 of your weekly hang sessions with at least one rest day between hard sessions</li>
        </ul>
        <p>The mixed grip protocol is common training practice and not a tested prescription.</p>
      </div>

      <p>Place mixed grip hangs after your standard <a href="/how-to-deadhang/">overhand dead hang</a> work. Double overhand remains the main grip for hang training. Mixed grip is a supplemental variation.</p>
      <p>Track hold times for each hand configuration separately. One configuration usually feels easier than the other. Start the first set with the harder configuration while your grip is freshest and alternate after that.</p>

      <h2>Common Mistakes</h2>
      <h3>Always Using the Same Hand Orientation</h3>
      <p>Training with the right hand always over and the left always under keeps each hand in the same position every time. One arm never practices the other position. Switch every set.</p>

      <h3>Gripping Too Narrow</h3>
      <p>A narrow grip can make the body twist more; a shoulder-width grip is easier to control. Grip at shoulder width or slightly wider.</p>

      <h3>Bending the Underhand Arm</h3>
      <p>The underhand hand tends to pull into a curl. Beginners often bend that elbow without noticing. Keep both arms straight. Any elbow bend turns the exercise into an uneven pull-up hold rather than a dead hang.</p>

      <h3>Ignoring Shoulder Position</h3>
      <p>Keep the shoulder blades down and the shoulders level so one side does not hunch.</p>
      <p>Check the <a href="/deadhang-form/">form guide</a> for detailed shoulder positioning cues.</p>

      <h3>Biceps Tendon Caution</h3>
      <p>Distal biceps tendon tears are associated with mixed-grip deadlifts and almost always affect the underhand arm. Keep the underhand elbow straight in the hang and stop if the front of the elbow hurts.</p>

      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <div class="faq">
        <h3>What is a mixed grip dead hang?</h3>
        <p>A mixed grip dead hang uses one hand overhand (pronated) and one hand underhand (supinated). The uneven grip can make the body want to turn. The trunk may work to stay straight. This has not been measured. A mixed grip dead hang is an optional variation. The main hang training should stay double overhand.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="faq">
        <h3>Should I switch hands during mixed grip hangs?</h3>
        <p>Yes. Alternate which hand is overhand and which is underhand every set of a mixed grip dead hang. Equal volume on both sides gives both arms practice in each position and keeps the training even.</p>
      </div>

